A window under an `if` that reads state is created when the condition turns
true and destroyed when it turns false, on gtk4 and fyne.

The window's position says whether it exists, the way an effect's does, so
passWindowLifetimes says it in passEffect's terms: the window moves to the
package's windows, and an effect takes its place whose mount and unmount are
`window.mount` and `window.unmount` on the window's handle -- `__win0` here,
since nothing names it. The effect settles wherever `details` is written, the
window's own `@close` included, which is how closing it writes the condition
back. The window's content is built once, with the rest, and outlives the
toplevel, so `n`'s updater in the main window's click patches a label that
exists whether or not the window is on screen.

An effect in the window's body lives as long as the window does: its
position starts with the window's condition (`__effect0` below reads
`details` before it reads `n`).

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        gtk4
        fyne
    }
}

var (
    details = false
    n = 0
    shown = 0
)

ui.window #main(title="Main") {
    ui.button(text="Details", @click {
        details = !details
    })
    ui.button(text="+", @click {
        n += 1
    })
    ui.text(value="shown {shown} times")
}

if details {
    ui.window(title="Load", @close {
        details = false
    }) {
        ui.text(value="{n}")
        effect(@mount {
            shown += 1
        })
    }
}
